[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Custom Commands and TypeScript Integration in Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cypress provides a powerful testing framework, and writing custom commands allows you to encapsulate repetitive actions into reusable functions. When combined with TypeScript, you also gain strict type safety, autocompletion, and robust refactoring support."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Project Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure you have Cypress installed with TypeScript support configured in your project."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Verify your tsconfig.json file includes Cypress types under the types array."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Creating a Custom Command"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a custom command, you typically add it to the cypress/support/commands.ts file. Let us create a custom login command that interacts with the UI."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Cypress.Commands.add('login', (email: string, password: string) => {
  cy.get('input[name="email"]').type(email);
  cy.get('input[name="password"]').type(password);
  cy.get('button[type="submit"]').click();
});"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Extending TypeScript Interfaces"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, TypeScript will throw an error when you try to use cy.login() because it does not know about the new command. To fix this, you must extend the Cypress namespace."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"declare namespace Cypress {
  interface Chainable {
    login(email: string, password: string): Chainable<Element>;
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Using the Custom Command in Tests"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now that your command is defined and typed, you can use it seamlessly inside your spec files with full autocompletion."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"describe('User Authentication', () => {
  it('should successfully log in using a custom command', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.login('user@example.com', 'securePassword123');
    cy.url().should('include', '/dashboard');
  });
});"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Integrating custom commands with TypeScript in Cypress drastically improves maintainability and developer experience. Always ensure your namespace declarations match the parameters and return types of your actual command implementations."}]
